WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.534 --> 00:00:03.534
-Our next guest is a very funny
comedian and writer,

00:00:03.534 --> 00:00:05.167
whose hit off-Broadway show
"Just For Us"

00:00:05.167 --> 00:00:07.434
is currently running at
the SoHo Playhouse.

00:00:07.434 --> 00:00:10.801
Please welcome to the show
Alex Edelman, everybody.

00:00:10.801 --> 00:00:13.334
[ Cheers and applause ]
♪♪

00:00:23.133 --> 00:00:25.701
Welcome to the show!
-Thanks so much for having me.

00:00:25.701 --> 00:00:27.467
Oh, my God.
-This is very exciting.

00:00:27.467 --> 00:00:29.267
You've done two runs
of your show.

00:00:29.267 --> 00:00:30.434
You're moving it to
a bigger theater

00:00:30.434 --> 00:00:31.801
this summer for six weeks.

00:00:31.801 --> 00:00:34.200
And you've had people like
Jerry Seinfeld in the house,

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:35.367
Billy Crystal in the house.

00:00:35.367 --> 00:00:37.300
What are -- What is it like --
excuse me --

00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:39.801
on those nights
when you know they're there?

00:00:39.801 --> 00:00:42.000
-I came in the night that
Seinfeld was coming,

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:43.100
and everyone was weird.

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:44.267
I was like, "What's going on?"

00:00:44.267 --> 00:00:45.634
And they're like,
"Jerry Seinfeld is coming."

00:00:45.634 --> 00:00:47.634
I was like,
"He's not coming to this show."

00:00:47.634 --> 00:00:49.901
And they're like,
"Yeah, no, Seinfeld's..."

00:00:49.901 --> 00:00:51.367
But he's -- It's amazing.

00:00:51.367 --> 00:00:52.634
And the show's producer,
Mike Birbiglia,

00:00:52.634 --> 00:00:54.033
who's one of my favorite comics.

00:00:54.033 --> 00:00:57.901
So it's like having one of
the heads of my Mount Rushmore

00:00:57.901 --> 00:00:59.901
of comedy producing the show,

00:00:59.901 --> 00:01:04.067
and then other heads from
the Mount Rushmore of comedy.

00:01:04.067 --> 00:01:07.234
And then, when Seinfeld came,
he said nice things afterwards.

00:01:07.234 --> 00:01:09.434
And then it was like,
"Yeah, but what's the thing

00:01:09.434 --> 00:01:13.267
that, like, you're going to get
back in the car and, like,

00:01:13.267 --> 00:01:15.868
look at your wife and go,
'That was good, but...'"

00:01:15.868 --> 00:01:20.167
And he went,
"I'm so glad you asked."

00:01:21.868 --> 00:01:23.334
So, yeah, I got notes
from Seinfeld.

00:01:23.334 --> 00:01:25.634
-You got some actual --
That's a very good way of

00:01:25.634 --> 00:01:28.367
asking for a real note,
because I think that that

00:01:28.367 --> 00:01:30.000
makes a person think,
"Oh, yeah, no,

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:32.234
this is what I'll say when I get
in the car, and it's safe."

00:01:32.234 --> 00:01:34.167
-Yeah, but it's a very, like --
But Billy Crystal's --

00:01:34.167 --> 00:01:37.901
The first show I ever --
The first solo show I ever saw

00:01:37.901 --> 00:01:42.167
was a Billy Crystal show in like
October of 2006 in Boston.

00:01:42.167 --> 00:01:43.934
And, like...
-Oh, that's pretty cool.

00:01:43.934 --> 00:01:46.400
So, now,
for those who don't know,

00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:50.067
this show is about a very --
a meeting you went to

00:01:50.067 --> 00:01:52.534
in New York City for a
very specific group of people.

00:01:52.534 --> 00:01:53.734
And I'm going to
let you explain it.

00:01:53.734 --> 00:01:57.467
-Yeah. Well, I'm Jewish,
and sometimes,

00:01:57.467 --> 00:01:58.901
people can't tell
that I'm Jewish

00:01:58.901 --> 00:02:01.601
because of my name or my face or
anything about my personality.

00:02:01.601 --> 00:02:07.200
But I got some anti-Semitism
on Twitter,

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:10.234
and so I started adding the
anti-Semites to, like,

00:02:10.234 --> 00:02:11.267
this list.

00:02:11.267 --> 00:02:12.701
You can make lists on Twitter.

00:02:12.701 --> 00:02:14.701
So I made this list,

00:02:14.701 --> 00:02:17.534
and I called it "Jewish National
Fund Contributors."

00:02:17.534 --> 00:02:18.734
-Gotcha.

00:02:18.734 --> 00:02:20.667
-Because every single time
I added someone,

00:02:20.667 --> 00:02:22.033
they'd see what it was.

00:02:22.033 --> 00:02:23.834
And, so, I started going down
this wormhole of, like,

00:02:23.834 --> 00:02:25.200
anti-Semites on the Internet.

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:27.834
And then, because of that,
I saw this thing that said,

00:02:27.834 --> 00:02:30.567
"Hey, you know,
if you live in New York City

00:02:30.567 --> 00:02:32.067
and you're curious about
your whiteness,

00:02:32.067 --> 00:02:35.934
come to this address in
this part of Queens."

00:02:35.934 --> 00:02:37.767
And I was like,
"I live in New York

00:02:37.767 --> 00:02:39.167
and I'm curious about
my whiteness.

00:02:39.167 --> 00:02:42.734
And I'm free
tomorrow night at 9:15."

00:02:42.734 --> 00:02:46.300
So I went to this meeting of
people who probably con--

00:02:46.300 --> 00:02:48.601
white nationalists -- people I'd
consider white nationalists.

00:02:48.601 --> 00:02:50.934
And I sat there for like
an hour and a half,

00:02:50.934 --> 00:02:52.467
and, eventually, someone's like,
"I'm sorry,

00:02:52.467 --> 00:02:54.167
but this guy --
I think this guy's a Jew."

00:02:54.167 --> 00:02:55.300
And I was like,
"Yeah, I'm a Jew."

00:02:55.300 --> 00:02:57.767
So, like, that's proudly
what this show is about.

00:02:57.767 --> 00:02:59.200
I'm like, "What? It's not
a big deal or anything."

00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:03.667
-But it sounds, like, so intense
and stressful to have done it.

00:03:03.667 --> 00:03:06.534
Was it -- I mean,
did you go into it terrified?

00:03:06.534 --> 00:03:07.701
-I mean,
I'm a stand-up comedian.

00:03:07.701 --> 00:03:08.834
-Yeah.

00:03:08.834 --> 00:03:10.400
-So, like,
I walk into rooms every night

00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:11.434
where people hate me.

00:03:11.434 --> 00:03:12.767
Like, that's a --

00:03:12.767 --> 00:03:14.000
-That's a good way of
looking at it.

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:16.434
-Yeah. Like, a hostile room
I got to win over?

00:03:16.434 --> 00:03:19.300
I'm like, "What is it? Tuesday?"
Like, "Yeah."

00:03:19.300 --> 00:03:21.000
That's how it works.

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:22.534
-And, so, then --
And did you know --

00:03:22.534 --> 00:03:25.133
While you were experiencing it,
did you sense,

00:03:25.133 --> 00:03:27.267
"Oh, this is going to be
something worth talking about"?

00:03:27.267 --> 00:03:28.367
-Not at all.

00:03:28.367 --> 00:03:29.467
Like, I have
a couple of friends --

00:03:29.467 --> 00:03:31.167
There was something
that happened,

00:03:31.167 --> 00:03:32.968
and I was like,
"My friend David's

00:03:32.968 --> 00:03:33.968
gonna think that's funny.

00:03:33.968 --> 00:03:35.501
And then
something else happened.

00:03:35.501 --> 00:03:36.734
I'm like, "My friend Morgan's --

00:03:36.734 --> 00:03:37.968
Morgan is going to
think that's funny."

00:03:37.968 --> 00:03:39.300
And then I was like,
"My friend Danny's

00:03:39.300 --> 00:03:40.968
going to think that's --"
And every single one of them,

00:03:40.968 --> 00:03:42.367
even the ones that weren't
stand-up comedians,

00:03:42.367 --> 00:03:44.634
were like, "You're talking about
this onstage, right?"

00:03:44.634 --> 00:03:46.167
And I was like,
"No, why would I?"

00:03:46.167 --> 00:03:48.167
And so, like,
it was the comedians

00:03:48.167 --> 00:03:50.067
and a couple friends
of mine who were like,

00:03:50.067 --> 00:03:51.601
"You have to start
talking about this."

00:03:51.601 --> 00:03:52.734
So, like, yeah, they pushed me
to make it into a thing.

00:03:52.734 --> 00:03:54.067
-The show is called
"Just For Us,"

00:03:54.067 --> 00:03:56.234
and do people -- When people
come to it, are they --

00:03:56.234 --> 00:03:59.200
have you had interactions with
the audience who maybe thought

00:03:59.200 --> 00:04:02.267
the show was something
they didn't expect?

00:04:02.267 --> 00:04:05.100
-This guy came up to me after
the show with his wife,

00:04:05.100 --> 00:04:08.033
and he's like, "You know,
that was not at all

00:04:08.033 --> 00:04:09.334
what we were expecting,
because we thought it was

00:04:09.334 --> 00:04:11.334
gonna be more like the TV show."

00:04:11.334 --> 00:04:13.701
And I was like, "What TV show?"

00:04:13.701 --> 00:04:16.167
And he's like, "The NBC show."

00:04:16.167 --> 00:04:20.133
And I was like, "They made this
into an NBC..."

00:04:20.133 --> 00:04:23.300
And he went, "Yeah,
the show about the family."

00:04:23.300 --> 00:04:25.100
And, you know, he's like,

00:04:25.100 --> 00:04:26.601
"And it's weird, because none of
them are Jewish."

00:04:26.601 --> 00:04:30.100
And I was like,
"That's 'This Is Us.'"

00:04:30.100 --> 00:04:32.100
[ Laughter ]

00:04:32.868 --> 00:04:33.934
-Yeah.

00:04:33.934 --> 00:04:35.501
-And he was like, "Yeah."

00:04:35.501 --> 00:04:38.734
And I was like, "That's not
the name of my show."

00:04:38.734 --> 00:04:42.400
And his wife went, "I told you."

00:04:42.400 --> 00:04:43.634
So it's like...

00:04:43.634 --> 00:04:44.834
-Like, there's still
a lot of things --

00:04:44.834 --> 00:04:47.000
Even if it was
the name of your show...

00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:48.033
-Yeah.

00:04:48.033 --> 00:04:49.734
-...who would go see
a one-man show

00:04:49.734 --> 00:04:51.133
about their favorite TV show?

00:04:51.133 --> 00:04:53.133
-Also, how can they
adapt it without

00:04:53.133 --> 00:04:54.667
Sterling K. Brown involved?

00:04:54.667 --> 00:04:56.067
-Yeah, exactly.

00:04:56.067 --> 00:04:59.067
That would be
a wildly offensive show, yeah.

00:04:59.067 --> 00:05:01.200
-Like, they made some
bold choices

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:02.801
for the stage adaptation.

00:05:02.801 --> 00:05:04.367
-All your comedian friends told
you, "Don't do that."

00:05:04.367 --> 00:05:05.734
-Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:05:05.734 --> 00:05:07.367
-"You should talk about
that white-nationalist thing.

00:05:07.367 --> 00:05:10.300
You can't do a one-man show of
'This Is Us.'"

00:05:10.300 --> 00:05:12.767
So, you are --
You're from Boston.

00:05:12.767 --> 00:05:14.667
We share a love of the Red Sox.

00:05:14.667 --> 00:05:15.801
-Ah, yeah.

00:05:15.801 --> 00:05:17.868
-I'm a fellow Steelers fan
with with Joe,

00:05:17.868 --> 00:05:19.033
and I'm a fellow Red Sox fan
with you.

00:05:19.033 --> 00:05:20.734
And you had a job
with the Red Sox.

00:05:20.734 --> 00:05:23.000
And based on
this I.D. card, Alex,

00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:25.534
I'm going to say you were a
young man when you had this job.

00:05:25.534 --> 00:05:28.567
There you go right there.

00:05:28.567 --> 00:05:29.901
So, what are we talking?
How old are you?

00:05:29.901 --> 00:05:31.033
And what's your job for
the Red Sox?

00:05:31.033 --> 00:05:32.434
-I mean, I got a job --

00:05:32.434 --> 00:05:34.300
The Red Sox were awesome,
and when I was a kid --

00:05:34.300 --> 00:05:35.767
I got a job there when I was 13.

00:05:35.767 --> 00:05:37.701
This is from when I was
16 or 17.

00:05:37.701 --> 00:05:38.801
-Gotcha.

00:05:38.801 --> 00:05:41.968
-I wrote the Red Sox
kids newsletter.

00:05:41.968 --> 00:05:43.133
-Kids newsletter?
-Yeah.

00:05:43.133 --> 00:05:44.934
But it was like, sometimes,
I'd ask questions

00:05:44.934 --> 00:05:47.767
in the press room, and, like...

00:05:47.767 --> 00:05:48.901
-You didn't have your own time.

00:05:48.901 --> 00:05:50.133
You'd be with
the regular reporters.

00:05:50.133 --> 00:05:52.133
-Sometimes, they'd allocate
me my own time,

00:05:52.133 --> 00:05:53.701
but sometimes, I'd be in
the pressroom with, like,

00:05:53.701 --> 00:05:55.567
the players, and they're like,
"So, you're --"

00:05:55.567 --> 00:05:57.467
There was one player who
was in a slump.

00:05:57.467 --> 00:06:00.067
He had gone 0 for 41.

00:06:00.067 --> 00:06:02.300
And they asked him about it.

00:06:02.300 --> 00:06:04.400
And I was like -- You know,
I'm 17, so I was like,

00:06:04.400 --> 00:06:06.200
"I'll raise the mood
a little bit."

00:06:06.200 --> 00:06:08.367
And so I raised my hand.

00:06:08.367 --> 00:06:10.767
And he was like, "Yeah?"

00:06:10.767 --> 00:06:13.400
And I said,
"Do you like bowling?"

00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:20.501
And he was like,
"Who the hell is this kid?"

00:06:20.501 --> 00:06:22.033
But it was great.
It was a great job.

00:06:22.033 --> 00:06:23.534
Baseball's my favorite sport.

00:06:23.534 --> 00:06:25.267
-I like that you thought that
that would, like, cheer him up.

00:06:25.267 --> 00:06:27.501
-I was like, "Well, someone
should ask him a question."

00:06:27.501 --> 00:06:28.634
-I mean, you know, obviously,

00:06:28.634 --> 00:06:30.133
he's not good at
baseball right now.

00:06:30.133 --> 00:06:31.868
What about the other sports?

00:06:31.868 --> 00:06:33.767
-It was like,
"When you go bowling,

00:06:33.767 --> 00:06:37.367
do you miss everything the way
you do at the plate?"

00:06:37.367 --> 00:06:39.601
-What's 0 for 41
in bowling like?

00:06:39.601 --> 00:06:42.767
-It's like 28 gutter balls
in a row.

00:06:42.767 --> 00:06:44.934
It was so -- It was the best --
I love baseball,

00:06:44.934 --> 00:06:46.834
because it's such a great sport,
especially if you like

00:06:46.834 --> 00:06:49.234
an hour of excitement,
you know, stretched out

00:06:49.234 --> 00:06:50.968
over the course of
two and a half years.

00:06:50.968 --> 00:06:54.000
And, so, like -- And it was
the most rewarding job.

00:06:54.000 --> 00:06:55.133
And, by the way, like,

00:06:55.133 --> 00:06:57.901
I saw you perform at
the Red Sox team dinner.

00:06:57.901 --> 00:06:59.501
-Yep, I did a team dinner for
the Red Sox -- very exciting.

00:06:59.501 --> 00:07:00.801
-Which was so cool.

00:07:00.801 --> 00:07:02.067
-And they won
the World Series the next year,

00:07:02.067 --> 00:07:04.734
and a lot of people said
it was that dinner was pretty...

00:07:04.734 --> 00:07:06.501
-Yeah, no, I heard that
around the office.

00:07:06.501 --> 00:07:08.734
People were
like, "It was all Seth."

00:07:08.734 --> 00:07:11.067
-But you --
We have a clip of you.

00:07:11.067 --> 00:07:14.467
Obviously, it was the ALCS.

00:07:14.467 --> 00:07:15.868
-I still get good seats,
so, yeah, I --

00:07:15.868 --> 00:07:17.534
-You got really good seats
at the ALCS.

00:07:17.534 --> 00:07:19.267
And the Red Sox
lost to the Astros.

00:07:19.267 --> 00:07:22.400
But you kind of came out
a winner, based on this clip.

00:07:22.400 --> 00:07:24.901
Now, just real quick,
before we set it up,

00:07:24.901 --> 00:07:26.367
based on the clip
we're about to see,

00:07:26.367 --> 00:07:31.400
it seems that you had a sense
that you could get on TV.

00:07:31.400 --> 00:07:35.067
-I just know when cameras
will cut to certain coaches.

00:07:35.067 --> 00:07:37.701
-Well, I will -- Can I just say,
the subtlety in it

00:07:37.701 --> 00:07:39.634
really shows that you have a
background in baseball,

00:07:39.634 --> 00:07:41.367
because I think
that there's a real --

00:07:41.367 --> 00:07:43.334
Just see if you can even notice
what Alex is doing here.

00:07:43.334 --> 00:07:44.667
Let's take a look.

00:07:44.667 --> 00:07:46.801
-It's hard to say that
four games into a postseason,

00:07:46.801 --> 00:07:49.234
but these are
the defending world champs,

00:07:49.234 --> 00:07:52.000
and they have had
their thumb on their opponents

00:07:52.000 --> 00:07:53.801
every game so far
in this postseason.

00:07:53.801 --> 00:07:55.901
-Hey, listen, no
one's defended since...

00:07:55.901 --> 00:07:57.367
-Very, very classy.

00:07:57.367 --> 00:07:58.734
Very -- Getting your head
in there.

00:07:58.734 --> 00:08:01.901
-Oh, yeah. Thank you.
You know, really spectacular.

00:08:01.901 --> 00:08:03.167
It's so fun.
-Yeah.

00:08:03.167 --> 00:08:04.567
Hey, congratulations so much
on the show.

00:08:04.567 --> 00:08:05.667
-Thanks, man.

00:08:05.667 --> 00:08:07.100
-I'm looking forward to
your summer run.

00:08:07.100 --> 00:08:08.634
You guys, that's Alex Edelman.

00:08:08.634 --> 00:08:10.434
"Just For Us,"
running now through April 30th

00:08:10.434 --> 00:08:11.467
at the SoHo Playhouse.

